    "sample_size": 13884,
    "key_findings": [
      "Common cold infections are mostly transmitted through hand-to-hand contact, not droplet spread. Children experience up to 5 colds annually, and adults 2-3, causing discomfort and time off work/school; most symptoms resolve within a week, but coughs often last longer.",
      "Nasal and oral decongestants provide short-term (3-10 hours) relief of nasal congestion, but their long-term effectiveness (>10 hours) is unknown.",
      "Antibiotics are generally ineffective for common cold symptoms, can cause adverse effects, and contribute to antibiotic resistance. They may only be beneficial in specific cases of bacterial co-infection, which are hard to identify.",
      "Vitamin C is unlikely to reduce the duration or severity of cold symptoms.",
      "The effectiveness of zinc gel or lozenges, echinacea, steam inhalation, and analgesics/anti-inflammatory drugs in reducing cold symptom duration is unknown."
    ],
    "conclusion": "In this systematic review we present information relating to the effectiveness and safety of the following interventions: analgesics or anti-inflammatory drugs, antibiotics, antihistamines, decongestants for short-term and for long-term relief, decongestants plus antihistamines, echinacea, steam inhalation, vitamin C, and zinc (intranasal gel or lozenges)."
  },
